course: dictionary
lesson: 3785
title: Footbridge and its clues
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A footbridge is a compound word: foot and bridge joined together. When a long word is made of smaller words you already know, the parts often reveal the meaning. A bridge for feet is a bridge for walkers.
explain: «A London footbridge wobbled on its first day in 2000 because walkers fell into step with its sway.» The clue is walkers. Ask yourself who is using the bridge. Walkers, not cars, so a footbridge is for people on foot.
explain: The dictionary confirms it: a footbridge is a narrow bridge only for people walking, not for cars. Try the swap test: put «a narrow bridge only for people walking» into the sentence. It still makes sense, so the meaning is right.
fact: A footbridge is a narrow bridge only for people walking, not for cars.
fact: Footbridges often cross railways, busy roads, rivers and streams, keeping walkers safe from traffic.
fact: Phrases you may meet are «cross the footbridge» and «a wooden footbridge».
fact: Footbridge is a noun, and its plural is footbridges.
fact: In design and technology, pupils build model footbridges to test how strong they are.
exercise: «The walkers crossed the footbridge over the busy road.» Where did the walkers go? | Under the road in a tunnel | Across the sea in a boat | *Over the road, on a bridge for people walking | Along the road in a car
exercise: Which word in «walkers fell into step with its sway» is a clue that the footbridge is for people on foot? | London | *walkers | 2000 | wobbled
exercise: In design and technology, why do pupils build model footbridges? | *To test how strong they are | To make them longer than real ones | Because real footbridges are not allowed | To see how fast cars can drive over them
test: What does the word footbridge mean? | A river boat | A railway track | *A narrow bridge only for people walking | A road for buses
test: Which phrase uses footbridge as something you cross? | drive the footbridge | *cross the footbridge | park the footbridge | swim the footbridge
exam: Split footbridge into two smaller words you know. What does the whole word mean? | *A bridge for walkers, made from foot and bridge | A bridge for cars | A path up a hill | A tunnel under a river
